 frequency policy uses the `scmi` or `qcom-cpufreq-hw` scaling driver and none
 offers an energy-performance preference:
 
@@ -291,13 +292,18 @@ scmi
 scmi
 ```
 
-The scaling governor follows the profile: `performance` in "performance", and
-`schedutil` in "balanced" and "power-saver", on policies that offer both.
-`schedutil` raises the frequency only as a task's tracked utilisation grows,
-over tens of milliseconds, so bursty work such as a web browser runs most of
-its bursts at low clocks: on a Snapdragon X2 Elite, Speedometer 3.1 scored 23.1
-under `schedutil` and 45.4 under `performance`. The minimum frequency is not
-changed. The caps and the governor are re-applied after resume. When the
+The scaling governor is `schedutil` in every profile, on policies that offer
+both `schedutil` and `performance`. `schedutil` raises the frequency only as a
+task's tracked utilisation grows, over tens of milliseconds, so bursty work
+such as a web browser runs most of its bursts at low clocks: on a Snapdragon X2
+Elite, Speedometer 3.1 scored 22 under `schedutil` and 47 under `performance`.
+But `performance` holds every core at full clock, idle ones included, which
+drew 2W more at idle, and the machine reset itself under load on battery.
+Omarchy instead gives the session's processes a utilisation clamp floor
+(`uclamp.min`) that follows the profile, 75% in "balanced" and 100% in
+"performance", which runs a core at least at the floor only while such a task
+is runnable: Speedometer 3.1 then scored 42 and 45. The minimum frequency is
+not changed. The caps and the governor are re-applied after resume. When the
 daemon stops, the caps are lifted to the hardware maximum and the governor is
 set back to `schedutil`, so another tool's lower `scaling_max_freq` or other
 governor is not preserved (see [Conflicts](#conflicts)).
diff --git a/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c b/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
index 3940c73..b465455 100644
--- a/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
+++ b/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
@@ -18,25 +18,36 @@
  *   Used where turning boost off is known to cap the CPU at its sustainable
  *   frequency. The governor follows the profile as with the caps below.
  *
- * - caps: each policy's scaling_max_freq, capped at a share of the policy's
- *   highest non-boost frequency, the way Windows' processor power management
- *   caps maximum performance per power mode. Used where boost is off by
- *   default or absent, so it can't tell the profiles apart. The boost switch
- *   is left alone: performance, which is uncapped, allows the boost
- *   frequencies, so whether they are used stays up to the switch. Their
- *   governor follows the profile too: performance in performance, and
- *   schedutil otherwise. schedutil raises the frequency only as a task's
- *   tracked utilisation grows, over tens of milliseconds, so bursty work such
- *   as a browser's runs most of its bursts at low clocks: Speedometer 3.1
- *   scored 23.1 under schedutil and 45.4 under performance on a Snapdragon X2
- *   Elite (Zenbook A16), against 48 on Windows.
+ * - caps: each policy's scaling_max_freq, capped in power-saver at a share
+ *   of the policy's highest non-boost frequency, the way Windows' processor
+ *   power management caps maximum performance per power mode. Used where
+ *   boost is off by default or absent, so it can't tell the profiles apart.
+ *   The boost switch is left alone: balanced and performance, which are
+ *   uncapped, allow the boost frequencies, so whether they are used stays up
+ *   to the switch.
+ *
+ *   Snapdragon X stays on schedutil in every profile. schedutil raises the
+ *   frequency only as a task's tracked utilisation grows, over tens of
+ *   milliseconds, so bursty work such as a browser's runs most of its bursts
+ *   at low clocks: Speedometer 3.1 scored 22 in balanced on a Snapdragon X2
+ *   Elite (Zenbook A16), against 47 with the performance governor. But that
+ *   governor holds every core at full clock, idle ones included: it drew 2W
+ *   more at idle, and the machine reset itself under load on battery. Omarchy
+ *   instead gives the session's processes a utilisation clamp floor that
+ *   follows the profile (omarchy-powerprofiles-floor), which runs a core at
+ *   least at the floor only while such a task is runnable: Speedometer 3.1
+ *   scored 42 in balanced with a 75% floor and 45 in performance with a 100%
+ *   floor. A 75% cap in balanced cost 6 of those points, so balanced is
+ *   uncapped too, and only the floor sets it apart from performance.
  *
  * An apple-cpufreq machine whose frequency table has no boost frequencies,
  * such as the M3 Max, has a boost switch the kernel refuses to turn on, or
  * none, so it uses the caps method instead. Only power-saver is capped there, which
  * keeps the boost method's split: balanced and performance run uncapped. Its
  * governor follows the profile as with any caps: on a MacBook Neo (A18 Pro),
- * Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8 under schedutil and 34.4 under performance.
+ * Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8 under schedutil and 34.4 under performance,
+ * so the performance governor stays in performance there, as with the boost
+ * method.
  *
  * It is only used when every cpufreq policy runs one of the scaling drivers
  * in the table below. Any other system keeps its current driver.
@@ -60,11 +71,10 @@
 #define CPUFREQ_POLICY_DIR "/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/"
 #define CPUFREQ_BOOST_PATH "/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/boost"
 
-/* Share of each policy's maximum frequency allowed per profile with the caps
- * method. Measured on a Snapdragon X Elite, 75% costs ~16% of sustained
- * throughput for ~39% less energy, and 60% ~37% for ~50% less. */
+/* Share of each policy's maximum frequency allowed in power-saver with the
+ * caps method. Measured on a Snapdragon X Elite, 60% costs ~37% of sustained
+ * throughput for ~50% less energy. */
 #define POWER_SAVER_PERCENT        60
-#define BALANCED_PERCENT           75
 #define UNCAPPED_PERCENT           100
 
 #define PERFORMANCE_GOVERNOR       "performance"
@@ -101,7 +111,7 @@ struct _PpdDriverCpufreq
 
   PpdProfile activated_profile;
   CpufreqMethod method;
-  guint balanced_percent; /* with the caps method */
+  gboolean performance_governor; /* in the performance profile */
   char *boost_path;
   GPtrArray *policies; /* of Policy, with the caps method */
 };
@@ -423,17 +433,16 @@ ppd_driver_cpufreq_probe (PpdDriver *driver)
   case CPUFREQ_METHOD_NONE:
     return PPD_PROBE_RESULT_FAIL;
   case CPUFREQ_METHOD_BOOST:
+    cpufreq->performance_governor = TRUE;
     if (boost_can_be_enabled (policy_dirs)) {
       ret = probe_boost (cpufreq);
       break;
     }
     g_debug ("No boost frequencies to switch, capping power-saver instead");
     cpufreq->method = CPUFREQ_METHOD_CAPS;
-    cpufreq->balanced_percent = UNCAPPED_PERCENT;
     ret = probe_caps (cpufreq, policy_dirs);
     break;
   case CPUFREQ_METHOD_CAPS:
-    cpufreq->balanced_percent = BALANCED_PERCENT;
     ret = probe_caps (cpufreq, policy_dirs);
     break;
   }
@@ -456,14 +465,12 @@ profile_to_boost (PpdProfile profile)
 }
 
 static guint
-profile_to_percent (PpdDriverCpufreq *cpufreq,
-                    PpdProfile        profile)
+profile_to_percent (PpdProfile profile)
 {
   switch (profile) {
   case PPD_PROFILE_POWER_SAVER:
     return POWER_SAVER_PERCENT;
   case PPD_PROFILE_BALANCED:
-    return cpufreq->balanced_percent;
   case PPD_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE:
     return UNCAPPED_PERCENT;
   }
@@ -517,14 +524,15 @@ write_cap (Policy   *policy,
 }
 
 static const char *
-profile_to_governor (PpdProfile profile)
+profile_to_governor (PpdDriverCpufreq *cpufreq,
+                     PpdProfile        profile)
 {
   switch (profile) {
   case PPD_PROFILE_POWER_SAVER:
   case PPD_PROFILE_BALANCED:
     return DEFAULT_GOVERNOR;
   case PPD_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE:
-    return PERFORMANCE_GOVERNOR;
+    return cpufreq->performance_governor ? PERFORMANCE_GOVERNOR : DEFAULT_GOVERNOR;
   }
 
   g_return_val_if_reached (DEFAULT_GOVERNOR);
@@ -624,7 +632,7 @@ apply_caps (PpdDriverCpufreq  *cpufreq,
 
   pick_up_new_policies (cpufreq);
 
-  percent = profile_to_percent (cpufreq, profile);
+  percent = profile_to_percent (profile);
   for (guint i = 0; i < policies->len;) {
     Policy *policy = g_ptr_array_index (policies, i);
     guint64 cap = policy_cap (policy, percent);
@@ -654,7 +662,7 @@ apply_caps (PpdDriverCpufreq  *cpufreq,
       i++;
       continue;
     }
-    write_governor (policy->dir, profile_to_governor (profile));
+    write_governor (policy->dir, profile_to_governor (cpufreq, profile));
     written++;
     i++;
   }
@@ -688,7 +696,7 @@ apply_boost (PpdDriverCpufreq  *cpufreq,
   if (!ppd_utils_write (cpufreq->boost_path, profile_to_boost (profile), error))
     return FALSE;
 
-  write_governors (profile_to_governor (profile));
+  write_governors (profile_to_governor (cpufreq, profile));
   return TRUE;
 }
 
